  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=sec\u2061x?$\n\n- The point $(0,2)$ is in the graph\n- $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote\n- $y=-2$ belongs to the range of the function.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot the graph of $y=sec\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934039/assets/da729794a0331383debd75be0a4ce4a9f2d4da3286f50bd4f284868996077412.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The $y$-intercept of $sec\u2061x$ is $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=sec\u2061x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ thus $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote.\n\n- Statement III is true. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ so $y=-2$ belongs to the range.",
